  was running. You'd get zero notifications for the exact case you most wanted them. Fixing that means reconciling
  against derived state on load — at which point you've written approach A anyway, [3mplus[0m a log, [3mplus[0m the code to keep
  them agreeing. Two sources of truth that can disagree is the bug factory here.

  [1mC. Derived set + stored dismissals.[0m Derive the live notification set from [38;5;153mtasks + now[39m, exactly as in A. Persist only a
  small set of dismissal keys: [38;5;153m${taskId}:${trigger}:${dueDateISO}[39m.

  [3mBuys:[0m A's self-healing correctness, plus the dismissal and unread behavior that makes a bell usable. The weekend case
  works, because the set is computed fresh on load and doesn't care what was running. And the composite key does
  something subtle and right: if you dismiss "due soon" and then [3mpush the due date out[0m, the key changes, so you get
  notified again for the new deadline — which is what you'd want, and which a plain [38;5;153mnotificationId[39m would get wrong.
  [3mCosts:[0m dismissals need pruning when tasks are deleted (small), and there's genuinely no history — you can't ask "what
  was overdue last Tuesday."

  [1mMy recommendation: C.[0m It's barely more code than A and avoids B's correctness hole entirely. It also migrates cleanly
  to the multi-user world you said you want to keep open: when a server exists, server-pushed events arrive as [3mstored[0m
  notifications and get merged into the same derived list at render time. The UI and the bell don't change at all — you
  add a source, not a rewrite. That's the property worth paying for, and C is the only one of the three that has it.

  The honest case against C: if you truly never add a server, the dismissal store is machinery you could have skipped
  with A.
